{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life, and the man became a living soul.</b> Rashi explained: even the beast has a living soul, but this is the life that is in all of them, for knowledge and speech were added in him. And his explanation is forced, for the verse does not explain חַיָּה as 'from all of them.' And in the Akeidah he explained that נִשְׁמַת חַיִּים — this is the vital spirit found in man first and foremost, and from its side the man would become a living soul once the second and third perfection were added in him, along the lines of נֶפֶשׁ, רוּחַ, and נְשָׁמָה. And the Rav thought that Rashi agreed with him. However, this explanation too is not fit to accept — that it should say נֶפֶשׁ חַיָּה about the ultimate perfection, given that this very expression is also said of beasts and wild animals. Therefore, to my mind it is not so; rather, one must explain the very opposite of their words, for נִשְׁמַת חַיִּים is the intellective, eternal soul. Go and learn who is doing the breathing and you will find that this speaks of the divine portion from above — and it says: even though HaShah breathed into man the intellective breath of life, nonetheless the man, at the beginning of his existence, was an ordinary living soul like the rest of the animals, for ״אָדָם עֲיִר פֶּרֶא יִוָּלֵד״ (Job 11:12), and the essence of his perfection depends on the diligence of his effort and the goodness of his choice once the eyes of his intellect open as he advances in years. But at the beginning of his existence, even though the breath of the spirit of life was already breathed into him, nonetheless the soul is not in him in actuality but only in potentiality — and if he does not gird his loins with zeal to go forth at the head of the vanguard to fight the battle of HaShem, then he remains in his beastliness and is likened to a beast. But an ox or a sheep or a goat when it is born — on that very day it is created with all its perfection, and there is no addition of perfection in it. And Scripture revealed this matter to us, so that man not delude himself into saying that without toil and labor he will attain the perfection that was already created with him, and rely on the superiority found in his formation — for the matter is not so; rather, everything depends on the labor of his hands, for it is always in his power to exchange and transform nature into intellect and intellect into nature. And for this reason it was not said in the formation of man, ״וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים כִּי טוֹב״, because at his creation his goodness and his beauty were not yet recognizable."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The reference וּבָעֲקֵדָה ('and in the Akeidah') is to Rabbi Isaac Arama's philosophical commentary Akeidat Yitzchak; 'הָרַב' later in the passage refers to the same author. The phrase אָדָם עֲיִר פֶּרֶא יִוָּלֵד is cited from Job 11:12."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:26:51.436139728Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}